@deffn Command vm-burst-digest &optional digest-type
@findex vm-burst-digest
Burst the current message (a digest) into its individual messages.
The digest’s messages are assimilated into the folder as new mail
would be.

Optional argument DIGEST-TYPE is a string that tells VM what kind
of digest the current message is.  If it is not given the value
defaults to the value of vm-digest-burst-type.  When called
interactively DIGEST-TYPE will be read from the minibuffer.

If invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
all marked messages will be burst.  If applied to collapsed
threads in summary and thread operations are enabled via
@code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the thread are
burst.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-burst-digest-to-temp-folder &optional digest-type
@findex vm-burst-digest-to-temp-folder
Burst the current message (a digest) into a temporary folder.
The digest’s messages are copied to a buffer and vm-mode is
invoked on the buffer.  There is no file associated with this
buffer.  You can use @code{vm-write-file} to save the buffer, or
@code{vm-save-message} to save individual messages to a real folder.

Optional argument DIGEST-TYPE is a string that tells VM what kind
of digest the current message is.  If it is not given the value
defaults to the value of vm-digest-burst-type.  When called
interactively DIGEST-TYPE will be read from the minibuffer.

If invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
all marked messages will be burst.  If applied to collapsed
threads in summary and thread operations are enabled via
@code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the thread are
burst.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-burst-mime-digest
@findex vm-burst-mime-digest
Burst a MIME digest
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-burst-rfc1153-digest
@findex vm-burst-rfc1153-digest
Burst an RFC 1153 style digest
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-burst-rfc934-digest
@findex vm-burst-rfc934-digest
Burst an RFC 934 style digest
@end deffn

@appendixsubsec User options

@defopt vm-burst-digest-messages-inherit-labels
@vindex vm-burst-digest-messages-inherit-labels
Non-nil values means messages from a digest inherit the digest’s labels.
Labels are added to messages with @code{vm-add-message-labels}, normally
bound to @code{l a}.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-digest-burst-type
@vindex vm-digest-burst-type
Value specifies the default digest type offered by @code{vm-burst-digest}
when it asks you what type of digest you want to unpack.  Allowed
values of this variable are:

@example
   "rfc934"
   "rfc1153"
   "mime"
   "guess"

@end example
rfc1153 digests have a preamble, followed by a line of exactly 70
dashes, with digested messages separated by lines of exactly 30 dashes.

rfc934 digests separate messages on any line that begins with a few
dashes, but doesn’t require lines with only dashes or lines with a
specific number of dashes.  In the text of the message, any line
beginning with dashes is textually modified to be preceded by a dash
and a space to prevent confusion with message separators.

MIME digests use whatever boundary that is specified by the
boundary parameter in the Content-Type header of the digest.

If the value is "guess", and you take the default
response when @code{vm-burst-digest} queries you, VM will try to guess
the digest type.

Default value: @code{"guess"}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-digest-center-preamble
@vindex vm-digest-center-preamble
Non-nil value means VM will center the preamble lines that precede
the start of a digest.  How the lines will be centered depends on the
ambient value of fill-column.   A nil value suppresses centering.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-digest-identifier-header-format
@vindex vm-digest-identifier-header-format
Header to insert into messages burst from a digest.
Value should be a format string of the same type as @code{vm-summary-format}
that describes a header to be inserted into each message burst from a
digest.  The format string must end with a newline.

Default value: @code{"X-Digest: %s\n"}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-digest-preamble-format
@vindex vm-digest-preamble-format
String which specifies the format of the preamble lines generated by
@code{vm-send-digest} when it is invoked with a prefix argument.  One
line will be generated for each message put into the digest.  See the
documentation for the variable @code{vm-summary-format} for information
on what this string may contain.  The format should *not* end
with nor contain a newline.

Default value: @code{"\"%s\" (%F)"}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-digest-send-type
@vindex vm-digest-send-type
String that specifies the type of digest @code{vm-send-digest} will use.
Legal values of this variable are:

"rfc934"
"rfc1153"
"mime"
nil

A nil value means to use plain text digests.

Default value: @code{"mime"}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-mime-digest-discard-header-regexp
@vindex vm-mime-digest-discard-header-regexp
Non-nil value should be a regular expression that tells
which headers should not appear in MIME digests created
by VM.  This variable along with @code{vm-mime-digest-headers}
determines which headers are kept and which are discarded.

If the value of @code{vm-mime-digest-discard-header-regexp} is nil, the headers
matched by @code{vm-mime-digest-headers} are the only headers that will be
kept.

If @code{vm-mime-digest-discard-header-regexp} is non-nil, then only
headers matched by this variable will be discarded; all others
will be kept.  @code{vm-mime-digest-headers} determines the order of
appearance in that case, with headers not matching any in the
@code{vm-mime-digest-headers} list appearing last in the headers
of the digestified messages.
@end defopt

@defopt vm-mime-digest-headers
@vindex vm-mime-digest-headers
List of headers that should be appear in MIME digests
created by VM.  These should be listed in the order you wish them
to appear in the messages in the digest.  Regular expressions are
allowed.  There’s no need to anchor patterns with "^", as
searches always start at the beginning of a line.  Put a colon at
the end of patterns to get exact matches.  (E.g. "Date" matches
"Date" and "Date-Sent".)  Header names are always matched
case insensitively.

If the value of @code{vm-mime-digest-discard-header-regexp} is nil, the headers
matched by @code{vm-mime-digest-headers} are the only headers that will be
kept.

If @code{vm-mime-digest-discard-header-regexp} is non-nil, then only
headers matched by that variable will be discarded; all others
will be kept.  @code{vm-mime-digest-headers} determines the order of
appearance in that case, with headers not matching any in the
@code{vm-mime-digest-headers} list appearing last in the headers
of the digestified messages.

Default value:
@example
("Resent-" "From:" "Sender:" "To:" "Newsgroups:" "Cc:" "Subject:"
 "Date:" "Message-ID:" "Keywords:" "MIME-Version:" "Content-")
@end example
@end defopt

@defopt vm-rfc1153-digest-discard-header-regexp
@vindex vm-rfc1153-digest-discard-header-regexp
Non-nil value should be a regular expression that tells
what headers should not appear in RFC 1153 digests created by VM.  This
variable along with @code{vm-rfc1153-digest-headers} determines which headers
are kept and which headers are discarded.

If the value of @code{vm-rfc1153-digest-discard-header-regexp} is nil, the headers
matched by @code{vm-rfc1153-digest-headers} are the only headers that will be
kept.

If @code{vm-rfc1153-digest-discard-header-regexp} is non-nil, then only
headers matched by this variable will be discarded; all others
will be kept.  @code{vm-rfc1153-digest-headers} determines the order of
appearance in that case, with headers not matching any in the
@code{vm-rfc1153-digest-headers} list appearing last in the headers of
the digestified messages.

Default value: @code{"\\(X400-\\)?Received:"}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-rfc1153-digest-headers
@vindex vm-rfc1153-digest-headers
List of headers that should be appear in RFC 1153 digests
created by VM.  These should be listed in the order you wish them
to appear in the digest.  Regular expressions are allowed.
There is no need to anchor patterns with "^", as searches always
start at the beginning of a line.  Put a colon at the end of
patterns to get exact matches.  (E.g. "Date" matches "Date"
and "Date-Sent".)  Header names are always matched case
insensitively.

If the value of @code{vm-rfc1153-digest-discard-header-regexp} is nil, the headers
matched by @code{vm-rfc1153-digest-headers} are the only headers that will be
kept.

If @code{vm-rfc1153-digest-discard-header-regexp} is non-nil, then only
headers matched by that variable will be discarded; all others
will be kept.  @code{vm-rfc1153-digest-headers} determines the order of
appearance in that case, with headers not matching any in the
@code{vm-rfc1153-digest-headers} list appearing last in the headers of
the digestified messages.

Default value:
@example
("Resent-" "Date:" "From:" "Sender:" "To:" "Newsgroups:" "Cc:"
 "Subject:" "Message-ID:" "Keywords:")
@end example
@end defopt

@defopt vm-rfc934-digest-discard-header-regexp
@vindex vm-rfc934-digest-discard-header-regexp
Non-nil value should be a regular expression that tells
what headers should not appear in RFC 934 digests created by VM.  This
variable along with @code{vm-rfc934-digest-headers} determines which headers
are kept and which are discarded.

If the value of @code{vm-rfc934-digest-discard-header-regexp} is nil, the headers
matched by @code{vm-rfc934-digest-headers} are the only headers that will be
kept.

If @code{vm-rfc934-digest-discard-header-regexp} is non-nil, then only
