will be kept.  @code{vm-rfc934-digest-headers} determines the order of
appearance in that case, with headers not matching any in the
@code{vm-rfc934-digest-headers} list appearing last in the headers
of the digestified messages.

Default value:
@example
("Resent-" "From:" "Sender:" "To:" "Newsgroups:" "Cc:" "Subject:"
 "Date:" "Message-ID:" "Keywords:")
@end example
@end defopt

@node Reference for Dired, Reference for Dispose, Reference for Digests, Reference
@appendixsec Dired
@cindex Dired

@appendixsubsec Commands

@deffn Command vm-dired-attach-file composition
@findex vm-dired-attach-file
Attach the file at point in the dired buffer to a VM composition
buffer as a mime attachment. 

The file is not inserted into the buffer and MIME encoded until
you execute @code{vm-mail-send} or @code{vm-mail-send-and-exit}.  A visible tag
indicating the existence of the object is placed in the
composition buffer.  You can move the object around or remove
it entirely with normal text editing commands.  If you remove the
object tag, the object will not be sent.

First argument COMPOSITION is the buffer into which the object
will be inserted.  When this function is called interactively
COMPOSITION’s name will be read from the minibuffer.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-dired-do-attach-files composition
@findex vm-dired-do-attach-files
Attach all marked files in the dired buffer to a VM composition
buffer as mime attachments. 

The files are not inserted into the buffer and MIME encoded until
you execute @code{vm-mail-send} or @code{vm-mail-send-and-exit}.  For each
file, a visible tag indicating the existence of the object is
placed in the composition buffer.  You can move the objects around
or remove them entirely with normal text editing commands.  If you
remove an object tag, the object will not be sent.

First argument COMPOSITION is the buffer into which the objects
will be inserted.  When this function is called interactively
COMPOSITION’s name will be read from the minibuffer.
@end deffn

@node Reference for Dispose, Reference for Editing messages, Reference for Dired, Reference
@appendixsec Dispose
@cindex Dispose

@appendixsubsec User options

@defopt vm-auto-folder-alist
@vindex vm-auto-folder-alist
Non-nil value should be an alist that VM will use to choose a default
folder name when messages are saved.  The alist should be of the form
((HEADER-NAME-REGEXP
@example
   (REGEXP . FOLDER) ... )
  ...)
@end example
where HEADER-NAME-REGEXP and REGEXP are strings, and FOLDER
is a string or an s-expression that evaluates to a string.

If any part of the contents of the first message header whose
name is matched by HEADER-NAME-REGEXP is matched by the regular
expression REGEXP, VM will evaluate the corresponding FOLDER
and use the result as the default folder for saving the message.
If the resulting folder name is a relative pathname, then it will
be rooted in the directory named by @code{vm-folder-directory}, or the
default-directory of the currently visited folder if
@code{vm-folder-directory} is nil.  If the resulting folder name is an IMAP
maildrop specification, then the corresponding IMAP folder is used for
saving. 

When FOLDER is evaluated, the current buffer will contain
only the contents of the header matched by HEADER-NAME-REGEXP.
It is safe to modify this buffer.  You can use the match data
from any \( ... \) grouping constructs in REGEXP along with the
function buffer-substring to build a folder name based on the
header information.  If the result of evaluating FOLDER is a
list, then the list will be treated as another auto-folder-alist
and will be descended recursively.

Whether REGEXP is matched case sensitively depends on the value
of the variable @code{vm-auto-folder-case-fold-search}.  Header names
are always matched case insensitively.
@end defopt

@defopt vm-auto-folder-case-fold-search
@vindex vm-auto-folder-case-fold-search
Non-nil value means VM will ignore case when matching header
contents while doing automatic folder selection via the variable
@code{vm-auto-folder-alist}.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-confirm-for-auto-archive
@vindex vm-confirm-for-auto-archive
Non-nil value causes VM to ask for confirmation when
@code{vm-auto-archive-messages} is invoked.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-delete-after-archiving
@vindex vm-delete-after-archiving
Non-nil value causes VM to automatically mark messages for deletion
after successfully auto-archiving them with the @code{vm-auto-archive-messages}
command.
@end defopt

@defopt vm-delete-after-bursting
@vindex vm-delete-after-bursting
Non-nil value causes VM automatically to mark a message for deletion
after it has been successfully burst by the @code{vm-burst-digest} command.
@end defopt

@defopt vm-delete-after-saving
@vindex vm-delete-after-saving
Non-nil value causes VM automatically to mark messages for deletion
after successfully saving them to a folder.
@end defopt

@defopt vm-edit-message-mode
@vindex vm-edit-message-mode
Major mode to use when editing messages in VM.

Default value: @code{text-mode}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-expunge-before-save
@vindex vm-expunge-before-save
Non-nil value causes VM to expunge deleted messages before
saving a folder.
@end defopt

@defopt vm-save-using-auto-folders
@vindex vm-save-using-auto-folders
Non-nil value means that VM will suggest folders for saving
messages automatically using the setting of @code{vm-auto-folder-alist}.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-visit-when-saving
@vindex vm-visit-when-saving
Value determines whether VM will visit folders when saving messages.
@code{Visiting} means that VM will read the folder into Emacs and append the
message to the buffer instead of appending to the folder file directly.
This behavior is ideal when folders are encrypted or compressed since
appending plaintext directly to such folders is a ghastly mistake.

A value of t means VM will always visit folders when saving.

A nil value means VM will never visit folders before saving to them, and
VM will generate an error if you attempt to save messages to a folder
that is being visited.  The latter restriction is necessary to insure
that the buffer and disk copies of the folder being visited remain
consistent.

A value other than nil or t means that VM will save to the folder
buffer if it is visited or to the file otherwise.

Default value: @code{not-always}
@end defopt

@node Reference for Editing messages, Reference for Encryption with EasyPG, Reference for Dispose, Reference
@appendixsec Editing messages
@cindex Editing messages

@appendixsubsec Commands

@deffn Command vm-discard-cached-data &optional count
@findex vm-discard-cached-data
Discard cached information about the current message.
When VM gathers information from the headers of a message, it stores it
internally for future reference.  This command causes VM to forget this
information, and VM will be forced to search the headers of the message
again for these data.  VM will also have to decide again which headers
should be displayed and which should not.  Therefore this command is
useful if you change the value of vm-visible-headers or
vm-invisible-header-regexp in the midst of a VM session.

Numeric prefix argument N means to discard data from the current message
plus the next N-1 messages.  A negative N means discard data from the
current message and the previous N-1 messages.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
data is discarded only from the marked messages in the current folder.
If applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread operations are
enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the
thread have their cached data discarded.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-edit-message &optional prefix-argument
@findex vm-edit-message
Edit the current message.  Prefix arg means mark as unedited instead.
If editing, the current message is copied into a temporary buffer, and
this buffer is selected for editing.  The major mode of this buffer is
controlled by the variable vm-edit-message-mode.  The hooks specified
in vm-edit-message-hook are run just prior to returning control to the user
for editing.

Use C-c ESC when you have finished editing the message.  The message
will be inserted into its folder replacing the old version of the
message.  If you don’t want your edited version of the message to
replace the original, use C-c C-] and the edit will be aborted.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-edit-message-abort
@findex vm-edit-message-abort
Abort the edit of a message, forgetting changes to the message.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-edit-message-end
@findex vm-edit-message-end
End the edit of a message and copy the result to its folder.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-edit-message-other-frame &optional prefix
@findex vm-edit-message-other-frame
Like vm-edit-message, but run in a newly created frame.
@end deffn

@node Reference for Encryption with EasyPG, Reference for Encryption with PGG (deprecated), Reference for Editing messages, Reference
@appendixsec Encryption with EasyPG
@cindex Encryption with EasyPG

@appendixsubsec Commands

@deffn Command vm-epg-ask-hook
@findex vm-epg-ask-hook
Ask whether to sign or encrypt outgoing messages with PGP/MIME.

Add to @code{vm-mail-send-hook} to be asked each time you send a message.
@code{vm-epg-ask-function} controls what is asked: by default
@code{vm-epg-prompt-for-action} offers a choice of actions, but it can also name
a single action to confirm, or your own function.

This hook must be last in @code{vm-mail-send-hook}, and signals an error if it is
not: signing covers the message as it stands, so a later hook that modified
the message would invalidate the signature.  Add it with the APPEND argument
to @code{add-hook}:

       (add-hook 'vm-mail-send-hook #'vm-epg-ask-hook t)
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-attach-public-key
@findex vm-epg-attach-public-key
Attach a public key to the composition as an application/pgp-keys part.
The key exported is the author’s, taken from the headers listed in
@code{vm-epg-get-author-headers}.  When that yields no address, or the variable
is nil, prompt for a user ID -- so any key in your keyring can be sent, not
only your own.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-cleartext-decrypt
@findex vm-epg-cleartext-decrypt
Decrypt the inline PGP message in the current message.
Replace the ASCII armor in a presentation copy with the plaintext, leaving
the folder unmodified, and report the outcome in the modeline.  A decryption
failure inserts the error text instead, faced with @code{vm-epg-error}.

If the plaintext is itself an inline signed message, verify it as well with
@code{vm-epg-cleartext-verify}.

Refuses to run on a read-only folder even though only the presentation copy
is written, unlike @code{vm-epg-cleartext-verify}, which does not check.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-cleartext-encrypt sign
@findex vm-epg-cleartext-encrypt
Encrypt the composition body in place as inline PGP ASCII armor.
With a prefix argument, SIGN non-nil, sign it as well.

This replaces the body with the armor rather than building a MIME structure
around it, so it cannot cover attachments; prefer @code{vm-epg-encrypt}, which
produces PGP/MIME.  Also used internally by @code{vm-epg-encrypt} to armor the
body it then wraps.

Every recipient must have a usable encryption key: with no recipient key
this signals an error rather than falling back to symmetric (passphrase)
encryption, which is never what is wanted for mail.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-cleartext-sign
@findex vm-epg-cleartext-sign
Sign the composition body in place as inline PGP ASCII armor.
This uses the OpenPGP cleartext signature framework, which leaves the text
readable and appends the signature to the body, rather than building a
multipart/signed MIME structure around it.  It therefore cannot cover
attachments; prefer @code{vm-epg-sign}, which produces PGP/MIME.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-cleartext-verify
@findex vm-epg-cleartext-verify
Verify the inline PGP signature in the current message.
Replace the ASCII armor in a presentation copy of the message with a
description of the signature, faced according to whether it verified, and
report the outcome in the modeline.  The folder itself is not modified.

If the signing key is not in your keyring and @code{vm-epg-fetch-missing-keys}
is non-nil, try to fetch it from a keyserver first.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-compose-mode &optional arg
@findex vm-epg-compose-mode
Minor mode for composing PGP/MIME messages with EPG.

Toggle the mode when ARG is nil, enable it when ARG is a positive number
and disable it otherwise.  Enabling makes the bindings below and the
PGP/MIME menu available in the composition buffer.

@example
Key             Binding
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
C-c # C-e       vm-epg-cleartext-encrypt
C-c # C-s       vm-epg-cleartext-sign
C-c # E         vm-epg-sign-and-encrypt
C-c # a         vm-epg-ask-hook
C-c # e         vm-epg-encrypt
C-c # k         vm-epg-attach-public-key
C-c # s         vm-epg-sign
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-compose-mode-menu arg1
@findex vm-epg-compose-mode-menu
PGP/MIME compose mode menu.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-encrypt &optional sign
@findex vm-epg-encrypt
Encrypt the composition as PGP/MIME, a multipart/encrypted message.
With a prefix argument, SIGN non-nil, sign it as well, which is what
@code{vm-epg-sign-and-encrypt} does.

Every recipient address found in the headers listed in
@code{vm-epg-get-recipients-headers} must have a usable encryption key in your
keyring; otherwise this signals an error and leaves the composition
untouched.  Note that the message is never encrypted to a passphrase: if no
recipient key can be found, it refuses rather than falling back to symmetric
encryption.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-insert-public-key
@findex vm-epg-insert-public-key
Insert a public key as ASCII armor into the composition at point.
The key is selected as for @code{vm-epg-attach-public-key}, but is inserted
inline rather than attached as a MIME part.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-prompt-for-action
@findex vm-epg-prompt-for-action
Prompt for a PGP action and return it.
The choices come from @code{vm-epg-prompt-action-alist}; RET repeats the previous
choice, and @code{q} aborts sending with an error.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-sign
@findex vm-epg-sign
Sign the composition with PGP/MIME, as a multipart/signed message.

RFC 3156 forbids 8bit content transfer encoding in signed messages, and
lines beginning with "From " must be armored, because a mail gateway that
re-encodes either one would invalidate the signature.

If the composition is not yet MIME-encoded, this encodes it in a
signature-safe way, using @code{vm-epg-sign-text-transfer-encoding} in place of
@code{vm-mime-8bit-text-transfer-encoding} and forcing
@code{vm-mime-composition-armor-from-lines} on; your normal settings for those
two variables therefore do not matter here.

If the composition has *already* been MIME-encoded -- for instance because
you encoded it yourself with @code{vm-mime-encode-composition} -- this cannot
re-encode it safely, so it checks for the two hazards above and refuses to
sign rather than produce a signature that breaks in transit.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-sign-and-encrypt
@findex vm-epg-sign-and-encrypt
Sign and encrypt the composition as PGP/MIME.
Equivalent to @code{vm-epg-encrypt} with a prefix argument.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-epg-snarf-keys
@findex vm-epg-snarf-keys
Import into your keyring the public keys in the body of the current message.
This treats the whole message body as key material, so it is meant for
messages that are an inline PGP public key block.  Keys arriving as an
application/pgp-keys MIME part are handled by
@code{vm-mime-display-internal-application/pgp-keys} instead.
@end deffn

@appendixsubsec User options

@defopt vm-epg-ask-function
@vindex vm-epg-ask-function
What @code{vm-epg-ask-hook} should do before sending a message.

The value is either an action symbol or a function:

@example
  nil                do nothing;
  ‘sign’             ask whether to sign;
  ‘encrypt’          ask whether to encrypt;
  ‘sign-and-encrypt’ ask whether to sign and encrypt;
  a function         called with no arguments, returning one of the action
                     symbols above, or nil for no action.

@end example
An action symbol ACTION selects the command @code{vm-epg-ACTION}, so any value
other than those listed must name an existing @code{vm-epg-} command.

Default value: @code{vm-epg-prompt-for-action}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-epg-auto-decrypt
@vindex vm-epg-auto-decrypt
If non-nil, decrypt encrypted messages automatically when displaying them.
When nil, a button is shown instead and decryption happens only when you
activate it.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-epg-auto-snarf
@vindex vm-epg-auto-snarf
If non-nil, snarf public keys automatically.
Snarfing means importing the public keys found in a message into your GnuPG
keyring.  When nil, a button is shown instead and keys are imported only
when you activate it.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-epg-cleartext-search-limit
@vindex vm-epg-cleartext-search-limit
Number of bytes to search into the message for a PGP clear text armor.

Default value: @code{4096}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-epg-fetch-missing-keys
@vindex vm-epg-fetch-missing-keys
If non-nil, fetch missing keys from a keyserver when verifying signatures.
When a signature was made by a key that is not in your keyring, contact the
keyserver configured for GnuPG to retrieve it and then verify again.  This
makes displaying a signed message reach out to the network.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-epg-get-author-headers
@vindex vm-epg-get-author-headers
The list of headers used to identify the author of an outgoing message.
The first address found in these headers is used to select the signing key.
If nil, the default EPG signing key is used.

Default value: @code{("From:" "Sender:")}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-epg-get-recipients-headers
@vindex vm-epg-get-recipients-headers
The list of headers used to identify the recipients of an outgoing message.
Every address found in these headers must have a usable encryption key, or
encryption fails.

Default value: @code{("To:" "CC:" "BCC:")}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-epg-sign-text-transfer-encoding
@vindex vm-epg-sign-text-transfer-encoding
The content transfer encoding used for signed MIME parts of type text.

RFC 3156 forbids 8bit encoding in signed messages, because a gateway that
re-encodes the body would invalidate the signature.  @code{vm-epg-sign}
therefore binds @code{vm-mime-8bit-text-transfer-encoding} to this value while
it encodes the composition, overriding your normal setting for the duration
of the signing operation.

Both choices are signature-safe; @code{quoted-printable} keeps mostly-ASCII text
readable to humans and to non-MIME tools, while @code{base64} is more compact for
text that is largely non-ASCII.

Default value: @code{quoted-printable}
@end defopt

@node Reference for Encryption with PGG (deprecated), Reference for External, Reference for Encryption with EasyPG, Reference
@appendixsec Encryption with PGG (deprecated)
@cindex Encryption with PGG (deprecated)

@appendixsubsec Commands

@deffn Command vm-pgg-ask-hook
@findex vm-pgg-ask-hook
Function to ask to sign or encrypt outgoing messages with PGP/MIME.

Put this function into @code{vm-mail-send-hook} to be asked each time you
send a message whether or not you want to sign or encrypt the
message. See @code{vm-pgg-ask-function} to determine which function is
proposed.

This hook should probably be the last of your hooks if you have several
other functions there.  Signing crucially relies on the fact that the
message is not altered afterwards. To put it into @code{vm-mail-send-hook}
put something like

       (add-hook 'vm-mail-send-hook #'vm-pgg-ask-hook t)

into your VM init file.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-attach-public-key
@findex vm-pgg-attach-public-key
Attach your public key to a composition.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-cleartext-decrypt
@findex vm-pgg-cleartext-decrypt
Decrypt the contents of the current message.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-cleartext-encrypt sign
@findex vm-pgg-cleartext-encrypt
Encrypt the composition as cleartext and with a prefix also SIGN it.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-cleartext-sign
@findex vm-pgg-cleartext-sign
Sign the message.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-cleartext-verify
@findex vm-pgg-cleartext-verify
Verify the signature in the current message.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-compose-mode &optional arg
@findex vm-pgg-compose-mode
Minor mode for interfacing with cryptographic functions.

Switch mode on/off according to ARG.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-compose-mode-menu arg1
@findex vm-pgg-compose-mode-menu
PGP/MIME compose mode menu.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-encrypt &optional sign
@findex vm-pgg-encrypt
Encrypt the composition as PGP/MIME.  With a prefix arg SIGN also sign it.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-prompt-for-action
@findex vm-pgg-prompt-for-action
Prompt for an action and return it. See also @code{vm-pgg-prompt-action-alist}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-sign
@findex vm-pgg-sign
Sign the composition with PGP/MIME.

If the composition is not encoded so far, it is encoded before signing.
Signing of already encoded messages is discouraged.

RFC 2015 and its successor 3156 forbid the use of 8bit encoding for signed
messages, but require to use quoted-printable or base64 instead.  Also lines
starting with "From " cause trouble and should be quoted.

Thus signing of encoded messages may cause an error.  To avoid this you must
set @code{vm-mime-8bit-text-transfer-encoding} to something different than 8bit and
@code{vm-mime-composition-armor-from-lines} to t.

The transfer encoding done by @code{vm-pgg-sign} can be controlled by the variable
@code{vm-pgg-sign-text-transfer-encoding}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-sign-and-encrypt
@findex vm-pgg-sign-and-encrypt
Sign and encrypt the composition as PGP/MIME.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-pgg-snarf-keys
@findex vm-pgg-snarf-keys
Snarf keys from the current message.
@end deffn

@appendixsubsec User options

@defopt vm-pgg-ask-function
@vindex vm-pgg-ask-function
The function to use in @code{vm-pgg-ask-hook}.

