@deffn Command vm-biff-fvwm-focus-vm-folder-frame
@findex vm-biff-fvwm-focus-vm-folder-frame
Jumps to the frame containing the folder for the selected message.

1) Your Emacs frame needs to have the folder name in its title, see the
   variable @code{frame-title-format} on how to set this up.

2) You need to define the FVWM2 function SelectWindow and start the
@example
   FvwmCommandS module.  Therefore, you will need the following lines
   in your .fvwm2rc file. 

@end example
AddToFunc InitFunction
+ I Module FvwmCommandS

AddToFunc RestartFunction
+ I Module FvwmCommandS

AddToFunc SelectWindow
+ I Next ($0) Iconify false
+ I Next ($0) Raise
+ I Next ($0) WarpToWindow 10p 10p
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-biff-mode &optional arg
@findex vm-biff-mode
A xlbiff like tool for VM: pop up a summary frame when mail arrives.

You should also set @code{vm-auto-get-new-mail}, since otherwise nothing goes
looking for new mail and this has nothing to pop up about.

This is a global minor mode.  If called interactively, toggle the
@code{Vm-Biff mode} mode.  If the prefix argument is positive, enable the
mode, and if it is zero or negative, disable the mode.

If called from Lisp, toggle the mode if ARG is @code{toggle}.  Enable the
mode if ARG is nil, omitted, or is a positive number.  Disable the mode
if ARG is a negative number.

To check whether the minor mode is enabled in the current buffer,
evaluate @code{(default-value 'vm-biff-mode)}.

The mode’s hook is called both when the mode is enabled and when it is
disabled.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-biff-popup &optional force
@findex vm-biff-popup
Scan the current VM folder for new messages and popup a summary frame.
@end deffn

