@deffn Command vm-visit-folder arg1 &rest rest
@findex vm-visit-folder
Visit a mail file.
VM will parse and present its messages to you in the usual way.

First arg FOLDER specifies the mail file to visit.  When this
command is called interactively the file name is read from the
minibuffer.

Prefix arg or optional second arg READ-ONLY non-nil indicates
that the folder should be considered read only.  No attribute
changes, messages additions or deletions will be allowed in the
visited folder.

The optional third arg JUST-VISIT (not available interactively)
says that the folder should be visited with as little initial
processing as possible.  No summary generation, no moving of the
message-pointer, no retrieval of new mail.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-folder-other-frame arg1 &rest rest
@findex vm-visit-folder-other-frame
Like vm-visit-folder, but run in a newly created frame.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-folder-other-window arg1 &rest rest
@findex vm-visit-folder-other-window
Like vm-visit-folder, but run in a different window.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-imap-folder arg1 &rest rest
@findex vm-visit-imap-folder
Visit a IMAP mailbox.
VM will present its messages to you in the usual way.  Messages
found in the IMAP mailbox will be downloaded and stored in a local
cache.  If you expunge messages from the cache, the corresponding
messages will be expunged from the IMAP mailbox when the folder is
saved. 

When this command is called interactively, the FOLDER name will
be read from the minibuffer in the format
"account-name:folder-name", where account-name is the short
name of an IMAP account listed in @code{vm-imap-account-alist} and
folder-name is a folder in this account.

Prefix arg or optional second arg READ-ONLY non-nil indicates
that the folder should be considered read only.  No attribute
changes, messages additions or deletions will be allowed in the
visited folder.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-imap-folder-other-frame arg1 &rest rest
@findex vm-visit-imap-folder-other-frame
Like vm-visit-imap-folder, but run in a newly created frame.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-imap-folder-other-window arg1 &rest rest
@findex vm-visit-imap-folder-other-window
Like vm-visit-imap-folder, but run in a different window.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-pop-folder arg1 &rest rest
@findex vm-visit-pop-folder
Visit a POP mailbox.
VM will present its messages to you in the usual way.  Messages
found in the POP mailbox will be downloaded and stored in a local
cache.  If you expunge messages from the cache, the corresponding
messages will be expunged from the POP mailbox.

First arg FOLDER specifies the name of the POP mailbox to visit.
You can only visit mailboxes that are specified in @code{vm-pop-folder-alist}.
When this command is called interactively the mailbox name is read from the
minibuffer.

Prefix arg or optional second arg READ-ONLY non-nil indicates
that the folder should be considered read only.  No attribute
changes, messages additions or deletions will be allowed in the
visited folder.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-pop-folder-other-frame arg1 &rest rest
@findex vm-visit-pop-folder-other-frame
Like vm-visit-pop-folder, but run in a newly created frame.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-pop-folder-other-window arg1 &rest rest
@findex vm-visit-pop-folder-other-window
Like vm-visit-pop-folder, but run in a different window.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-thunderbird-folder arg1 &rest rest
@findex vm-visit-thunderbird-folder
Visit a mail file maintained by Thunderbird.
VM will parse and present its messages to you in the usual way.

First arg FOLDER specifies the mail file to visit.  When this
command is called interactively the file name is read from the
minibuffer.

Prefix arg or optional second arg READ-ONLY non-nil indicates
that the folder should be considered read only.  No attribute
changes, messages additions or deletions will be allowed in the
visited folder.

This function differs from @code{vm-visit-folder} in that it remembers that
the folder is a foreign folder maintained by Thunderbird.  Saving
of messages is carried out preferentially to other Thunderbird folders.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-virtual-folder folder-name &optional read-only bookmark summary-format directory
@findex vm-visit-virtual-folder
Visit the virtual folder FOLDER-NAME.  With a prefix argument,
visit it in read-only mode.

When called in Lisp code, additional optional arguments BOOKMARK and
SUMMARY-FORMAT specify the message where the pointer should be and the
summary format to use.  DIRECTORY is the default directory for the
virtual folder buffer.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-virtual-folder-other-frame folder-name &optional read-only bookmark summary-format directory
@findex vm-visit-virtual-folder-other-frame
Like @code{vm-visit-virtual-folder}, but run in a newly created frame.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-visit-virtual-folder-other-window folder-name &optional read-only bookmark summary-format directory
@findex vm-visit-virtual-folder-other-window
Like @code{vm-visit-virtual-folder}, but run in a different window.
@end deffn

@appendixsubsec User options

@defopt vm-toolbar
@vindex vm-toolbar
Non-nil value should be a list of toolbar button descriptors.
See the documentation for the variable default-toolbar for a
definition of what a toolbar button descriptor is.

If @code{vm-toolbar} is set non-nil VM will use its value as a toolbar
instantiator instead of the usual behavior of building a button
list based on the value of @code{vm-use-toolbar}.  @code{vm-use-toolbar} still
must be set non-nil for a toolbar to appear, however.

Consider this variable experimental; it may not be supported forever.
@end defopt

@node Reference for Virtual folder actions, Reference for Virtual folders, Reference for VM itself, Reference
@appendixsec Virtual folder actions
@cindex Virtual folder actions

@appendixsubsec Commands

@deffn Command vm-add-spam-word word
@findex vm-add-spam-word
Add a new WORD to the list of spam words.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-avirtual-check-for-missing-selectors &optional arg
@findex vm-avirtual-check-for-missing-selectors
Check if there are selectors missing for either vm-mode or mail-mode.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-sort-insert-auto-folder-names
@findex vm-sort-insert-auto-folder-names
Head each run of messages in the summary with the folder it would be filed to.
Called interactively it sorts the folder by auto-folder first, so that the
messages destined for one folder are together, and then writes that
folder’s name above each run.  The names are display only: they are removed
and rewritten each time, and no message is changed.

Which folder a message would go to is @code{vm-virtual-auto-select-folder}’s
answer, from @code{vm-virtual-auto-folder-alist}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-spam-words-rebuild
@findex vm-spam-words-rebuild
Discharge the internal cached data about spam words.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-apply-function count &optional selector function
@findex vm-virtual-apply-function
Apply a FUNCTION to the next COUNT messages matching SELECTOR.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-auto-archive-messages &optional prompt
@findex vm-virtual-auto-archive-messages
With a prefix ARG ask user before saving.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-auto-delete-message &optional count selector
@findex vm-virtual-auto-delete-message
Mark messages matching a virtual folder selector for deletion.
The virtual folder selector can be configured by the variable
@code{vm-virtual-auto-delete-message-selector}.

This function does not visit the virtual folder, but checks only the current
message, therefore it is much faster and not so disturbing like the method
described in the VM-FAQ.

In order to automatically mark spam for deletion use the function
@code{vm-virtual-auto-delete-messages}.  See its documentation on how to hook it
into VM!
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-auto-delete-messages
@findex vm-virtual-auto-delete-messages
Mark all messages from the current up to the last for (spam-)deletion.
Add this to @code{vm-arrived-messages-hook}.

See the function @code{vm-virtual-auto-delete-message} for details.

 (add-hook 'vm-arrived-messages-hook #'vm-virtual-auto-delete-messages)
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-check-selector-interactive selector &optional diagnostics
@findex vm-virtual-check-selector-interactive
Return t if SELECTOR matches the current message.
Called with an prefix argument we display more diagnostics about the selector
evaluation.  Information is displayed in the order of evaluation and indented
according to the level of recursion. The displayed information is has the
format: 
	FATHER-SELECTOR: RESULT CHILD-SELECTOR
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-filter-messages &optional count
@findex vm-virtual-filter-messages
Apply @code{vm-virtual-filter-alist} to the next COUNT messages.
Messages matched by a rule with @code{:skip-inbox} are expunged once every
rule has run.  Returns the number of messages some rule matched.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-filter-new-messages
@findex vm-virtual-filter-new-messages
Apply @code{vm-virtual-filter-alist} to the messages that have just arrived.
Add this to @code{vm-arrived-messages-hook}:

 (add-hook 'vm-arrived-messages-hook #'vm-virtual-filter-new-messages)

Like @code{vm-virtual-auto-delete-messages}, this runs from the current
message to the last, which on arrival is exactly the new mail.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-get-selector vfolder &optional valid-folder-list
@findex vm-virtual-get-selector
Return the selector of virtual folder VFOLDER for VALID-FOLDER-LIST.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-make-folder-persistent
@findex vm-virtual-make-folder-persistent
Save all messages of current virtual folder in the real folder
with the same name.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-omit-message &optional count message-list
@findex vm-virtual-omit-message
Omits a message from a virtual folder.
IMHO allowing it for real folders makes no sense.  One rather should create a
virtual folder of all messages.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-save-message &optional folder count
@findex vm-virtual-save-message
Save the current message to a mail folder.
Like @code{vm-save-message} but the default folder is guessed by
@code{vm-virtual-auto-select-folder}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command vm-virtual-update-folders &optional count message-list
@findex vm-virtual-update-folders
Add the current message to all virtual folders that are
applicable.  

With a prefix argument COUNT, the current message and the next
COUNT - 1 messages are added.  A negative argument means
the current message and the previous |COUNT| - 1 messages are
added.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
only marked messages are added, other messages are ignored.  If
applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread operations are
enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the
thread are added.
@end deffn

@appendixsubsec User options

@defopt vm-virtual-auto-delete-message-expunge
@vindex vm-virtual-auto-delete-message-expunge
When true we expunge the affected right after marking and saving them.
@end defopt

@defopt vm-virtual-auto-delete-message-folder
@vindex vm-virtual-auto-delete-message-folder
When set to a folder name we save affected messages there.
@end defopt

@defopt vm-virtual-auto-delete-message-selector
@vindex vm-virtual-auto-delete-message-selector
Name of virtual folder selector used for automatically deleting a message.
Actually they are only marked for deletion.

Default value: @code{"spam"}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-virtual-auto-folder-alist
@vindex vm-virtual-auto-folder-alist
Non-nil value should be an alist that VM will use to choose a default
folder name when messages are saved.  The alist should be of the form
@example
        ((VIRTUAL-FOLDER-NAME . FOLDER-NAME)
          ...)
@end example
where VIRTUAL-FOLDER-NAME is a string, and FOLDER-NAME
is a string or an s-expression that evaluates to a string.

This allows you to extend @code{vm-virtual-auto-select-folder} to generate
a folder name.  Your function may use @code{folder} to get the currently chosen
folder name and @code{mp} (a vm-message-pointer) to access the message. 

Example:
 (setq vm-virtual-auto-folder-alist
@example
       '(("spam" (concat folder "-"
                           (format-time-string "%y%m" (current-time))))))

@end example
This will return "spam-0008" as a folder name for messages matching the
virtual folder selector of the virtual folder "spam" during August in year
2000.
@end defopt

@defopt vm-virtual-check-case-fold-search
@vindex vm-virtual-check-case-fold-search
Whether virtual selectors match without regard to case.
Applies to the selectors run by the combinators @code{vm-vs-and}, @code{vm-vs-or} and
@code{vm-vs-not}, and by their mail-mode counterparts in vm-avirtual.el.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@defopt vm-virtual-check-diagnostics
@vindex vm-virtual-check-diagnostics
Non-nil means print what each virtual selector decided, and why.
The combinators print a line per selector as they evaluate it, indented by
depth; @code{vm-virtual-check-selector-interactive} turns this on for one call when
given a prefix argument.

Lives here rather than in vm-avirtual.el, which defines the rest of that
feature, because the combinators that read it are in vm-virtual.el.
@end defopt

@defopt vm-virtual-filter-alist
@vindex vm-virtual-filter-alist
Rules deciding what happens to a message when it arrives.
Non-nil value should be an alist of the form

@example
        ((VIRTUAL-FOLDER-NAME . ACTIONS)
          ...)

@end example
where VIRTUAL-FOLDER-NAME names a virtual folder in
@code{vm-virtual-folder-alist}, whose selector says which messages the rule
applies to, and ACTIONS is a property list of what to do with them:

@example
  :label STRING       attach the labels named in STRING, which is a
                      space separated list, as ‘vm-add-message-labels’
                      takes them
  :attributes STRING  set the attributes named in STRING, a space
                      separated list of ‘vm-supported-attribute-names’,
                      as ‘vm-set-message-attributes’ takes them
  :save FOLDER        save a copy in FOLDER.  FOLDER is a string or an
                      expression evaluating to one
  :skip-inbox t       keep the message out of the folder: it is flagged
                      deleted and expunged once every rule has run

@end example
Every rule that matches is applied, in the order they appear here, so a
message can be labelled by one rule and saved by another.

To have the rules run on incoming mail:

 (add-hook 'vm-arrived-messages-hook #'vm-virtual-filter-new-messages)

The message is written into the folder before any of this happens, so
@code{:skip-inbox} removes it again rather than preventing its arrival.

An example, taking two rules from @code{vm-virtual-folder-alist}:

 (setq vm-virtual-folder-alist
@example
       '(("from-arik" (("inbox") (author "arik")))
         ("spam"      (("inbox") (spam-word)))))
@end example
 (setq vm-virtual-filter-alist
@example
       '(("from-arik" :label "arik" :attributes "read")
         ("spam"      :save "spam-folder" :skip-inbox t)))
@end example
@end defopt

@defopt vm-virtual-make-up-auto-folder-names
@vindex vm-virtual-make-up-auto-folder-names
Non-nil value causes the vm-avirtual.el package to make up
auto-folder names from virtual folder names, so that all messages
belonging to a virtual folder are saved to real folders with the
same name.  Any auto-folder names suggested in
@code{vm-virtual-auto-folder-alist} will take priority over such made
up names.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

